\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\theta - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}-\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0-\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{16} + \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31881f34-a432-59b0-8b38-3684f13c3b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{4} + \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561274} {:answer/id #uuid "422e23d8-7878-5ea2-9a27-766bea4c942a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561278} {:answer/id #uuid "4d95f346-a420-5191-8ec2-3e7926314a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{16} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561282} {:answer/id #uuid "f673e57e-d0b6-5998-93a3-565e128e73e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561305} {:answer/id #uuid "fe398fb7-a1e5-5acd-a33a-c5efe140276d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "422e23d8-7878-5ea2-9a27-766bea4c942a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561278} :answer-field/id #uuid "45be7b21-96cb-50e1-a316-7060f7c9bd71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eacc9511-7fa5-53af-97be-32d0cbc36aff" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 3 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 3 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = 3\\left[\\theta + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}]}
